#---
# purpose: "a closure delivered by the mailbox drives Enum.each, whose step throws the call's result away"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# fz-kdt.155. `Enum.each/2` is the one Enum higher-order call that DISCARDS what
# the callable returns: `each_step` calls `fun.(entry)` for its effect and hands
# the accumulator on. A mailbox callable is reached through the boxed apply
# seam, and the callsite that reaches it there names none of the wrapper's
# members, so its discard never reaches them: the wrapper hands a value back
# and the callsite has to receive one however little it wants it. Publishing
# no delivered lanes there compiled the two halves of one calling convention
# against different lane counts: the
# continuation read the returned value as its own closure pointer and the
# native run aborted in `fz_closure_get_capture_atom` after the FIRST element.
#
# Three shapes drive the seam from different directions: a bare mailbox lambda,
# one carrying a capture of its own, and the empty list as the control that
# never reaches a step at all. `:done` proves control came back past every one.
fn main() do
  send(self(), fn (x) -> dbg(x * 2) end)
  double = receive do f -> f end
  Enum.each([1, 2, 3], double)

  scale = 10
  send(self(), fn (x) -> dbg(x * scale) end)
  scaled = receive do f -> f end
  Enum.each([4, 5], scaled)

  Enum.each([], double)
  dbg(:done)
end
